<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sapieha family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Sapieha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sapiehas are a Lithuanian and Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> family of Ruthenian origin that rose to prominence in the sixteenth century. Lew Sapieha was chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and editor of the Third Statute of 1588. In the later seventeenth century the family dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, until a confederation of other nobles broke their power in 1700.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sapieha-family.jpg" alt="Sapieha family" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape by Bastianowa (Bastiana) . · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POL_COA_Sapieha_alt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
